(Discrepancy 5 - minimal repro. LANGUAGE.md:1939 says to group arithmetic
 subexpressions with curly braces, and :1826 shows `{x add y} multiply z`. The
 Grammar Summary at :5233 instead gives `primary ::= literal | identifier |
 func_call | "(" expr ")"`, which claims parentheses group.
 They do not: parentheses are comment delimiters, so `(x add y)` is a comment
 and the rest of the sentence loses its subject.
 The recorded output is the brace form working; the commented line is the
 grammar's parenthesised form, rejected with "Expected a statement, got
 Multiply".
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D5.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   20
)
a number called x is 2.
a number called y is 3.
a number called z is 4.
Print {x add y} multiply z.

(Rejected: Expected a statement, got Multiply.)
(Print (x add y) multiply z.)

